.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-SEND-BATCH 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-send-message-batch \- send several messages to a queue in one call
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs send-message-batch
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I NAME\fR|\fIERN
.BR \-\-messages | \-m
.I JSON\fR|\fIfile://PATH
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sends several messages to one queue in a single call.
.PP
.B \-\-messages
is a JSON array of objects, each carrying the fields
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-send-message (1)
takes for one message \-
.BR body ,
and optionally
.BR attributes ,
.B systemAttributes
and
.BR priority .
The queue is named once, so every message in a batch goes to the same queue.
.PP
The saving over calling send-message in a loop is mostly in the database,
not the network. Each single send is its own round trip; a batch is written
in one insert, and the queue's counters are adjusted once rather than once
per message. A thousand documents measured 29 ms inserted together against
13,621 ms one at a time.
.SH A REJECTED MESSAGE DOES NOT STOP THE OTHERS
A message that cannot be sent \- a body over the queue's limit, a priority
that is not one of the three \- is reported and the rest are still sent.
The response says how many were asked for and how many went, lists the ids
of those that went in request order, and names each rejection by its
.I position in the array you sent:
.RS
.nf
{
  "ern": "ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:orders",
  "asked": 100,
  "sent": 99,
  "messageIds": ["a1b2...", "c3d4...", ...],
  "failed": [
    {"index": 37, "reason": "message is 2097152 bytes, and this queue accepts 1048576"}
  ]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is deliberately unlike
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-objects (1),
which only counts what it did. A delete that skipped a key removed
something that was already gone; a send that skipped a message dropped it.
A producer holding "99 of 100" and no way to learn which one would have to
send all hundred again and duplicate ninety-nine of them.
.PP
Every message being rejected is still a 200 with a reason for each \- the
request was well formed and has been answered. The command exits non-zero
in that case, so a script that checks only the exit status does not read it
as success.
.SH LIMITS
An installation accepts at most
.B euclid.modules.eqs.max-batch-size
messages per call, 100 by default. An empty array, or one over the cap, is
refused outright with 400: those are mistakes in the request rather than in
a message, and there is no partial outcome to report.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send three messages:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eqs send-message-batch --queue orders --messages '[
  {"body": "first"},
  {"body": "second", "priority": "HIGH"},
  {"body": "third", "attributes": {"source": {"type": "string", "value": "import"}}}
]'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send a batch prepared earlier:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eqs send-message-batch --queue orders --messages file://batch.json
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-send-message (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue (1)
